/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A charge moving at steady speed carries its field lines with it and nothing leaves.
   - In a wire of length l, l dI/dt = q dv/dt: a changing current is accelerating charge.
   - On a two-wire line the currents in the two close wires are opposite, so their fields cancel at a distance.
   - The magnetic vector potential A at a point P sums every bit of current in the antenna, each taken as it was R/c seconds earlier, R being its distance from P; hence “retarded”.
   - A = (μ₀/4π) ∫ J e−jβR / R dV′.
   - Once A is known, the fields follow: H = (1/μ₀) ∇ × A, and away from the source E comes from Maxwell’s equation E = (1/jωε₀) ∇ × H, with ε₀ = 8.854 × 10⁻¹² F/m and ω = 2πf.
   - Put an element of length l ≪ λ at the origin along z, carrying a uniform peak current I₀.
   - The brackets hold terms in 1/r, 1/r² and 1/r³.
   - Far away (βr ≫ 1) only the 1/r terms are left, Er vanishes and the far field is Eθ = j η β I₀ l sin θ e−jβr / (4πr), with Hφ = Eθ/η.
   - The average power density is S = |Eθ|²/(2η) W/m².
*/
