/* book exam traps: Using Rr = 80π²(l/λ)² for a short dipole with triangular current. That formula is for uniform current (the Hertzian dipole); a real short dipole gives 20π²(l/λ)², four times less. | Keeping 1/r² or 1/r³ terms in a far-field answer, or dropping them close in. The far field is the 1/r term only, and only once βr ≫ 1. | Drawing the pattern with its maximum along the wire. The sin θ factor makes the axis a null and broadside the maximum. | Using P = I₀² Rr with a peak current. With peak I₀ the power is ½ I₀² Rr; the ½ goes only if I is an rms value. */
