/* book exam traps: Taking half power where the field is 0.5. Half power is where the field is 0.707 (power 0.5); field 0.5 is the −6 dB point. | Using D ≈ 41 253/(θE θH) with angles in radians, or for a broad or multi-lobe beam. It needs degrees and one pencil beam, and even then it is an estimate. | Writing G > D. Gain is directivity times an efficiency of at most 1, so G ≤ D; “lossless” in a question means G = D. | Adding dBi values to plain ratios. Convert first: 3 dBi is a ratio of 2, not 3, and G = ηrad D is done in ratios, then converted. | Calling the back lobe “all the minor lobes”. Minor lobes are every lobe except the main one; the back lobe is only the one opposite the main beam. */
